vd-rd downstream fork architecture
===================================

This is a downstream vendor fork of upstream U-Boot, maintained for vd-rd's
board fleet across multiple SoC vendors. Reference for the fork's structure.
Everything below is vendor-agnostic policy unless a section says otherwise.

Branches
--------

- ``main`` -- pure mirror of upstream ``master``. Fast-forward only, never
  receives vendor commits directly.
- ``vendor/release`` -- vendor patch series rebased onto each upstream
  *final* release tag only (``vYYYY.MM`` / ``vYYYY.MM.N``, never ``-rcN``).
  Release-flavour builds come from here.
- ``vendor/dev`` -- vendor patch series rebased onto upstream ``main`` HEAD
  regularly. Dev-flavour (bleeding edge / testing) builds come from here.
  Board and feature work happens on short-lived topic branches off this
  branch, merged in via PR.

An ``upstream`` remote (``https://github.com/u-boot/u-boot.git``, the
official read-only mirror of ``source.denx.de/u-boot/u-boot``) is used to
pull new upstream commits/tags.

Upstream sync
-------------

``.github/workflows/sync-upstream.yml`` runs weekly (and on demand via
``workflow_dispatch``):

1. Fast-forwards ``main`` to ``upstream/main``.
2. Rebases ``vendor/dev`` onto ``upstream/main``.
3. On a new upstream *final* tag, rebases ``vendor/release`` onto it.
4. On any rebase conflict, aborts and files a GitHub issue instead of
   force-pushing a broken branch.

Note: scheduled (``cron``) workflows only fire from the repository's
*default* branch on GitHub. Since ``main`` must stay untouched, the GitHub
repo's default branch should be set to ``vendor/dev`` for the schedule to
actually run -- this is a one-time manual setting change (Settings ->
Branches on GitHub), not something this repo's contents control.

Versioning
----------

U-Boot's ``Makefile`` reserves ``SUBLEVEL`` for downstream forks. On
``vendor/release``, after rebasing onto a new upstream final tag, ``SUBLEVEL``
is set/bumped per vendor-only patch revision and the result is tagged
``vYYYY.MM.<N>`` (e.g. ``v2026.10.1``). ``vendor/dev`` isn't tagged --
U-Boot's own git-describe-based local version string plus the CI artifact
name (``<defconfig>-dev-<date>-<shortsha>``) is enough to identify a build.

Board namespace
---------------

New boards live under ``board/vd-rd/<board-name>/``, regardless of SoC
vendor (mirroring upstream's own ``board/<vendor>/<name>/`` layout:
``Kconfig``, ``Makefile``, ``MAINTAINERS``, ``<name>.c``).

Devicetree sourcing
--------------------

DTS files for vd-rd boards are **not** kept in this repo. They're
maintained in a separate coordinator repo shared with the Linux kernel tree
(single source of truth for the same hardware description), compiled there,
and the resulting ``.dtb`` is placed at a fixed raw offset on the boot
medium -- the same way the environment already lives at a fixed offset.

U-Boot proper is configured with ``CONFIG_OF_BOARD`` (not ``OF_SEPARATE``,
which is layout-coupled to this repo's own build via ``fdt_find_separate()``)
plus ``OF_HAS_PRIOR_STAGE``/``OF_OMIT_DTB`` so the build produces no linked
or appended DTB at all. ``board_fdt_blob_setup()`` returns a pointer to an
FDT already resident in DRAM rather than fetching it itself -- it runs
before driver-model bring-up, which is itself driven by the control FDT, so
it can't do a DM-based storage read. Who places that FDT in DRAM (a prior
boot stage vs. SPL vs. something else) is vendor/SoC-specific -- see below.

Out of scope so far
--------------------

- Actual ``board/vd-rd/...`` and devicetree content (needs schematics).
- Per-vendor ``board_fdt_blob_setup()`` loader implementations.
- The coordinator repo itself, and its dispatch integration with this
  fork's CI (``# TODO: dispatch to coordinator`` stubs are left in the
  build workflows for this).

Microchip/Atmel AT91
=====================

First boards targeted: ``glasnost_m9260``, ``glasnost_m9g10``,
``glasnost_m9g20`` (classic AT91SAM9, four separate PCB designs) and
``glasnost_m9g25`` (AT91SAM9x5 family). Everything in this section is
specific to this vendor/SoC family -- don't assume it generalizes.

Classic AT91SAM9 boards in this fleet use AT91Bootstrap as the first-stage
bootloader, external to this repo. AT91Bootstrap performs SoC clock/SDRAM
init and loads ``u-boot.bin`` directly into SDRAM -- there is no U-Boot SPL
stage at all (confirmed for ``glasnost_m9260``; presumed, not yet verified,
for the other three boards in this family). ``u-boot.bin`` is built with
``CONFIG_SKIP_LOWLEVEL_INIT`` and the normal build-time-embedded devicetree
(default ``OF_CONTROL``/``OF_SEPARATE`` flow, dtb appended to the image) --
AT91Bootstrap loads the combined image as a single blob, so there is no
separate DTB-at-a-fixed-offset handoff to design for this family.

(An earlier version of this doc claimed classic AT91SAM9 SPL has no driver
model / devicetree support at all, as the justification for the fixed-offset
DTB handoff plan below it. That claim was an overstatement in general --
``board/siemens/taurus`` proves ``SPL_DM`` + ``SPL_OF_PLATDATA`` fits in this
SoC family's tiny SPL SRAM budget upstream -- and moot for this fleet's
boards specifically, since they don't use U-Boot SPL at all.)

Boot-medium fallback (MMC normal boot, SPI-NOR recovery) is handled by
U-Boot's Standard Boot (``CONFIG_BOOTSTD_FULL``), not by a
defconfig-per-medium split or an SPL-level device order: the ``boot_targets``
environment variable (e.g. ``"mmc0 spi_flash0"``) gives U-Boot proper an
ordered list of bootdevs to scan, falling through to the next one if nothing
bootable is found. The SPI-NOR side needs ``CONFIG_BOOTDEV_SPI_FLASH`` +
``CONFIG_BOOTMETH_SCRIPT``, which reads a boot script from a fixed
``script_offset_f``/``script_size_f`` env-configured offset in SPI flash --
this is the recovery image's mechanism, distinct from AT91Bootstrap's own
boot-medium selection (which decides where ``u-boot.bin`` itself is loaded
from, a separate, earlier and so far unexamined question). Whether
AT91Bootstrap itself supports a similar try-this-then-that medium fallback
for loading U-Boot proper hasn't been investigated yet.
